// `Self` in the signature of a method on a GENERIC extension (DF-216r).
//
// `extension Wrap<T> { func same(&self, other: &Self) }` reported ``argument
// `other` expects `&Self` but got `&Wrap<Int>```. DF-216f fixed the
// non-generic spelling and deliberately left this one, because there are TWO
// `Self`s in a generic extension and it only had one:
//
//   * the RECEIVER's, which `_ext_self_type` keeps ARGUMENT-FREE (`Wrap`, not
//     `Wrap<T>`) on purpose — naming the extension's own parameters as
//     arguments makes a payload binding and a `T` parameter resolve through
//     different routes to two `T`s that do not unify, and codegen names the
//     concrete monomorphization from `self_type_context` instead;
//   * the one a SIGNATURE WRITES, which cannot use that spelling: substituting
//     a bare `Wrap` into a signature where only `Wrap$1$Int` was ever
//     monomorphized turned the clean error above into `internal compiler
//     error: Undefined struct: Wrap` (verified while fixing DF-216f, which is
//     why `_self_type_is_substitutable` exists).
//
// The written one is the extension APPLIED TO ITS OWN PARAMETERS — `Wrap<T>`,
// exactly what a user may write by hand — so the receiver's type arguments
// substitute it at the call site through machinery that already existed. The
// matrix over every position is `self_type_generic_extension_positions.saw`.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: same 9

struct Wrap<T> { v: T }

extension Wrap<T> {
    func same(&self, other: &Self) -> Int { 9 }
}

func main() {
    let w = Wrap<Int>(v: 0)
    print("same {w.same(other: &w)}")
}
